::ALG1_U01_L05_Quiz_Q01::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L05_Q01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.FND.4<br><br>Question:<br>In the expression 9x + 4, what is the variable? {
~A. 9#This is the coefficient, not the variable. The variable is the letter that represents a quantity.
=B. x#Correct. The variable is x because it represents a quantity that can change.
~C. 4#This is the constant because it stands alone.
~D. 9x#This is a term, not just the variable.
}

::ALG1_U01_L05_Quiz_Q02::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L05_Q02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.FND.4<br><br>Question:<br>In the expression 9x + 4, what is the coefficient? {
=A. 9#Correct. The coefficient is the number multiplied by the variable x.
~B. x#This is the variable. The coefficient is the number attached to the variable.
~C. 4#This is the constant because it stands alone.
~D. 9x#This is the full variable term. The coefficient is only the numerical factor.
}

::ALG1_U01_L05_Quiz_Q03::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L05_Q03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.FND.4<br><br>Question:<br>In the expression 9x + 4, what is the constant? {
~A. 9#This is the coefficient. It is multiplied by x.
~B. x#This is the variable. A constant is a number that stands alone.
=C. 4#Correct. The 4 stands alone, so it is the constant.
~D. 9x#This is a variable term. It includes the coefficient and the variable.
}

::ALG1_U01_L05_Quiz_Q04::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L05_Q04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.FND.4<br><br>Question:<br>Which are the terms in 6n - 5? {
~A. 6 and n#These are factors in the variable term, not the terms of the full expression.
~B. n and 5#This misses the coefficient attached to n. The variable term is 6n.
~C. 6 and 5#This identifies numbers but not the variable term.
=D. 6n and 5#Correct. The expression has the terms 6n and 5.
}

::ALG1_U01_L05_Quiz_Q05::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L05_Q05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.FND.4<br><br>Question:<br>Which expression has a coefficient of 12? {
~A. x + 12#The 12 is a constant because it stands alone.
=B. 12x + 5#Correct. The 12 is multiplied by x, so it is the coefficient.
~C. 5x + 12#The coefficient is 5. The 12 is the constant.
~D. x - 12#The 12 is a constant being subtracted, not a coefficient.
}

::ALG1_U01_L05_Quiz_Q06::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L05_Q06<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.FND.4<br><br>Question:<br>Which expression has a constant of 7? {
~A. 7x + 2#The 7 is a coefficient because it is multiplied by x.
~B. x + 2#The constant is 2, not 7.
=C. 4x + 7#Correct. The 7 stands alone, so it is the constant.
~D. 7(x + 2)#The 7 is a factor outside the grouped quantity, not a standalone constant.
}

::ALG1_U01_L05_Quiz_Q07::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L05_Q07<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.FND.4<br><br>Question:<br>A student earns $15 per hour. In the expression 15h, what does h represent? {
=A. the number of hours worked#Correct. The coefficient 15 is the hourly pay, so h represents hours worked.
~B. the hourly pay#This describes the coefficient 15, not the variable h.
~C. the total pay#The whole expression 15h represents total pay.
~D. the fixed fee#There is no fixed fee shown in 15h.
}

::ALG1_U01_L05_Quiz_Q08::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L05_Q08<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.FND.4<br><br>Question:<br>A student earns $15 per hour. In the expression 15h, what does 15 represent? {
~A. the number of hours#The variable h represents the number of hours.
=B. the hourly pay rate#Correct. The 15 is the coefficient and represents dollars per hour.
~C. the total pay#The expression 15h represents total pay, not the coefficient alone.
~D. a one-time fee#A one-time fee would be a constant added to the expression.
}

::ALG1_U01_L05_Quiz_Q09::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L05_Q09<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.FND.4<br><br>Question:<br>A rental company charges $25 per day plus a $40 fee. Which expression represents the total cost for d days? {
~A. 25 + 40d#This makes 40 depend on the number of days. The daily charge is $25.
~B. 25d#This leaves out the fixed $40 fee.
=C. 25d + 40#Correct. 25d represents the daily cost, and 40 represents the fixed fee.
~D. 40d + 25#This reverses the daily charge and fixed fee.
}

::ALG1_U01_L05_Quiz_Q10::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L05_Q10<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.FND.4<br><br>Question:<br>In 25d + 40, what does 40 represent if a rental company charges $25 per day plus a $40 fee? {
~A. the number of days#The variable d represents the number of days.
~B. the daily charge#The coefficient 25 represents the daily charge.
~C. the total cost#The full expression 25d + 40 represents the total cost.
=D. the fixed fee#Correct. The 40 stands alone, so it represents the fixed fee.
}

::ALG1_U01_L05_Quiz_Q11::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L05_Q11<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.FND.4<br><br>Question:<br>In 3(x + 5), which part is the grouped quantity? {
~A. 3#This is a factor outside the parentheses.
~B. x#This is only one part of the grouped quantity.
=C. x + 5#Correct. The parentheses group x + 5 as one quantity.
~D. 5#This is only one part of the grouped quantity.
}

::ALG1_U01_L05_Quiz_Q12::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L05_Q12<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.FND.4<br><br>Question:<br>What does 3(x + 5) mean structurally? {
~A. 3 plus the quantity x + 5#The 3 is written next to the parentheses, which means multiplication.
=B. 3 groups of the quantity x + 5#Correct. The expression means 3 multiplied by the grouped quantity x + 5.
~C. x plus 15 only#This multiplies only 5 by 3 and ignores that x + 5 is grouped.
~D. 3x plus 5 only#This multiplies only x by 3 and ignores the grouped quantity.
}

::ALG1_U01_L05_Quiz_Q13::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L05_Q13<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.FND.4<br><br>Question:<br>Which expression has one term at the outer level? {
~A. 4x + 1#This has two terms separated by addition.
~B. 7n - 3#This has two terms separated by subtraction.
=C. 5(x + 2)#Correct. At the outer level, this expression is one product.
~D. 9a + 4#This has two terms separated by addition.
}

::ALG1_U01_L05_Quiz_Q14::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L05_Q14<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.FND.4<br><br>Question:<br>Which expression has two terms at the outer level? {
~A. 6(x + 1)#This is one product at the outer level.
=B. 4m + 9#Correct. The terms are 4m and 9.
~C. 8p#This is one term.
~D. 2(a + 5)#This is one product at the outer level.
}

::ALG1_U01_L05_Quiz_Q15::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L05_Q15<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.FND.4<br><br>Question:<br>In the equation 10s + 30 = 150, what could 10s represent if s is the number of shirts and each shirt costs $10? {
~A. the fixed fee#The fixed fee would be represented by the standalone 30.
~B. the total cost#The full left side 10s + 30 represents the total before comparing to 150.
=C. the cost of the shirts#Correct. 10s means $10 times the number of shirts.
~D. the number of shirts#The variable s represents the number of shirts.
}

::ALG1_U01_L05_Quiz_Q16::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L05_Q16<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.FND.4<br><br>Question:<br>In the equation 10s + 30 = 150, what could 150 represent? {
~A. the cost per shirt#The cost per shirt is represented by 10.
~B. the number of shirts#The number of shirts is represented by s.
~C. the fixed fee#The fixed fee is represented by 30.
=D. the total amount#Correct. In this context, 150 can represent the total amount.
}

::ALG1_U01_L05_Quiz_Q17::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L05_Q17<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.FND.4<br><br>Question:<br>A student says the coefficient in 8x + 3 is 3. Which correction is best? {
=A. The coefficient is 8 because it is multiplied by x.#Correct. The coefficient is the number attached to the variable term.
~B. The coefficient is x because x changes.#This identifies the variable. The coefficient is the numerical factor.
~C. The coefficient is 8x because it is a term.#This identifies the full term. The coefficient is only the number factor.
~D. The coefficient is 11 because 8 + 3 = 11.#This adds parts of the expression instead of identifying the coefficient.
}

::ALG1_U01_L05_Quiz_Q18::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L05_Q18<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.FND.4<br><br>Question:<br>A student says 6p means 6 + p. Which correction is best? {
~A. 6p means 6 - p.#This changes the operation incorrectly. A number next to a variable means multiplication.
=B. 6p means 6 multiplied by p.#Correct. A coefficient next to a variable means multiplication.
~C. 6p means p divided by 6.#This reverses the meaning. The notation shows multiplication, not division.
~D. 6p means p is always 6.#This confuses a variable expression with a variable value.
}

::ALG1_U01_L05_Quiz_Q19::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L05_Q19<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.FND.4<br><br>Question:<br>Which statement correctly describes 2b + 9? {
~A. b is the coefficient.#b is the variable. The coefficient is the number attached to b.
~B. 9 is the coefficient.#9 is the constant because it stands alone.
=C. 2 is the coefficient and 9 is the constant.#Correct. The 2 is multiplied by b, and 9 stands alone.
~D. 2b and 9 are factors.#2b and 9 are terms at the outer level, not factors of one product.
}

::ALG1_U01_L05_Quiz_Q20::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L05_Q20<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.FND.4<br><br>Question:<br>Which statement correctly describes 7(y + 4)? {
~A. y + 4 is a constant.#The expression y + 4 includes a variable, so it is not a constant.
~B. 7 is the only term inside the parentheses.#The 7 is outside the parentheses.
~C. y and 4 cannot be viewed together.#The parentheses show that y + 4 should be viewed together.
=D. y + 4 is a grouped quantity multiplied by 7.#Correct. The parentheses group y + 4 as one quantity.
}

::ALG1_U01_L05_Quiz_Q21::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L05_Q21<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.FND.4<br><br>Question:<br>A concert ticket costs $35 plus a $6 processing fee. In 35t + 6, what does 35t represent? {
=A. the cost of t tickets#Correct. The expression 35t means $35 times the number of tickets.
~B. the processing fee#The processing fee is represented by the constant 6.
~C. the number of tickets#The variable t represents the number of tickets.
~D. the total cost including the fee#The full expression 35t + 6 represents the total cost including the fee.
}

::ALG1_U01_L05_Quiz_Q22::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L05_Q22<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.FND.4<br><br>Question:<br>A concert ticket costs $35 plus a $6 processing fee. In 35t + 6, what does the full expression represent? {
~A. the number of tickets#The variable t represents the number of tickets.
~B. the ticket price only#The term 35t represents the ticket cost for t tickets.
=C. the total cost including the fee#Correct. 35t gives the ticket cost, and + 6 adds the processing fee.
~D. the processing fee only#The processing fee is represented by the constant 6.
}

::ALG1_U01_L05_Quiz_Q23::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L05_Q23<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.FND.4<br><br>Question:<br>Which expression best represents “$4 per notebook plus a $2 fee,” using n for notebooks? {
~A. 4 + 2n#This makes the $2 fee depend on the number of notebooks. The $4 is the per-notebook amount.
=B. 4n + 2#Correct. 4n represents $4 per notebook, and 2 represents the fee.
~C. 2n + 4#This reverses the per-notebook cost and fee.
~D. 4n - 2#The context says plus a $2 fee, not subtracting $2.
}

::ALG1_U01_L05_Quiz_Q24::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L05_Q24<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.FND.4<br><br>Question:<br>Which part of 12 + 5p is the variable term? {
~A. 12#This is the constant because it stands alone.
~B. 5#This is the coefficient, not the full variable term.
~C. p#This is the variable only. The variable term includes the coefficient and variable.
=D. 5p#Correct. The variable term is 5p because it contains the variable p.
}

::ALG1_U01_L05_Quiz_Q25::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L05_Q25<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.FND.4<br><br>Question:<br>Which interpretation of 4(x + 3) is most accurate? {
~A. 4 plus the quantity x + 3#The 4 is multiplied by the grouped quantity, not added.
~B. x plus 12 only#This multiplies only 3 by 4 and ignores the grouped structure.
=C. 4 groups of the quantity x + 3#Correct. The parentheses show x + 3 as one quantity, and 4 multiplies that quantity.
~D. 4x plus 3 only#This multiplies only x by 4 and ignores that x + 3 is grouped.
}
